 then forgets about it","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsW"},{"id":"CQLt1uwhHnvy-fCIqPZaT","type":"text","x":-3847.166929001238,"y":4147.661547761547,"width":527.28,"height":16.25,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1336812031,"version":106,"versionNonce":168853599,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"ASYNC  -  every consumer with its own promise and its own right to fall behind","originalText":"ASYNC  -  every consumer with its own promise and its own right to fall behind","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsX"},{"id":"7wgWD14xaoj9__L29PT3t","type":"text","x":-3037.166929001238,"y":4147.661547761547,"width":256.88,"height":16.25,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":783780383,"version":106,"versionNonce":1031633169,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"READ PATH  -  nothing is computed here","originalText":"READ PATH  -  nothing is computed here","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsY"},{"id":"ww4Hqrh49Roeu4ierbyAA","type":"text","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":5725.661547761547,"width":977.6,"height":25,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":345959999,"version":106,"versionNonce":2113870975,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE FLOWS  -  where each one starts, what it touches, where it ends, and what makes it a cycle","originalText":"THE FLOWS  -  where each one starts, what it touches, where it ends, and what makes it a cycle","fontSize":20,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsZ"},{"id":"TcHoSZPLQQyI-I_uwTEgj","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":5771.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1816448607,"version":106,"versionNonce":980299505,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsa"},{"id":"zU8qcH3imbQlakUD3fhkj","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":5787.661547761547,"width":218.4,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1887021695,"version":106,"versionNonce":235222175,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"1.  THE TELEMETRY WRITE FLOW","originalText":"1.  THE TELEMETRY WRITE FLOW","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsb"},{"id":"xlRmyLWLm_Ftybp8vVp_P","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":5819.661547761547,"width":486.2,"height":288.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":771621535,"version":106,"versionNonce":663562449,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  an agent wakes 30 seconds after it last slept, plus a random smear so that\n        100 million machines never knock at the same instant. it reads battery, cpu,\n        disk and uptime, stamps the reading with its OWN event id and sequence\n        number, and gzips it.\n\nTHEN    the ingest gateway is the first thing it touches, and it checks three things\n        in this order and no other:  is this device quarantined (one set lookup, no\n        parsing at all)  ->  is its api key valid  ->  is it inside its token bucket.\n        any of those failing returns 429 with a Retry-After. never a 500.\n\nTHEN    Ingest Service adds OUR clock as receivedAt, asks the guard rail whether it\n        has already seen this event id in the last ten minutes, and produces one\n        record onto telemetry.raw, keyed by deviceId.\n\nENDS    the moment Kafka acknowledges, the agent gets 202 with nextPushAfterSec.\n        about 30 ms end to end. nothing downstream has run yet, and the agent\n        neither knows nor cares.\n\nCYCLE   the agent sleeps for nextPushAfterSec and does it all again. if the 202\n        never arrived it retries with the SAME event id, which is exactly why a\n        retry is harmless rather than a second reading.","originalText":"STARTS  an agent wakes 30 seconds after it last slept, plus a random smear so that\n        100 million machines never knock at the same instant. it reads battery, cpu,\n        disk and uptime, stamps the reading with its OWN event id and sequence\n        number, and gzips it.\n\nTHEN    the ingest gateway is the first thing it touches, and it checks three things\n        in this order and no other:  is this device quarantined (one set lookup, no\n        parsing at all)  ->  is its api key valid  ->  is it inside its token bucket.\n        any of those failing returns 429 with a Retry-After. never a 500.\n\nTHEN    Ingest Service adds OUR clock as receivedAt, asks the guard rail whether it\n        has already seen this event id in the last ten minutes, and produces one\n        record onto telemetry.raw, keyed by deviceId.\n\nENDS    the moment Kafka acknowledges, the agent gets 202 with nextPushAfterSec.\n        about 30 ms end to end. nothing downstream has run yet, and the agent\n        neither knows nor cares.\n\nCYCLE   the agent sleeps for nextPushAfterSec and does it all again. if the 202\n        never arrived it retries with the SAME event id, which is exactly why a\n        retry is harmless rather than a second reading.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsc"},{"id":"WWjPD9xA__Rp6Ea97MndM","type":"rectangle","x":-4177.166929001238,"y":5771.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1166169791,"version":106,"versionNonce":1104365759,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsd"},{"id":"VMkh86XgoM5yrn1x5NoJs","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":5787.661547761547,"width":366.6,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1839671007,"version":106,"versionNonce":1852958385,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"2.  THE CONFIG FLOW  -  and the emergency brake","originalText":"2.  THE CONFIG FLOW  -  and the emergency brake","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bse"},{"id":"iwfKD7HYQZ2OmJNietopD","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":5819.661547761547,"width":474.76,"height":220,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":888992511,"version":106,"versionNonce":1460263135,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  the agent boots, or an hour has passed since it last asked.\n\nTHEN    it asks the CDN for its manifest: which datapoints to send, how often, what\n        sampling to apply. 100 M agents asking hourly is ~28 000 requests a second,\n        and not one of them reaches us. the edge answers.\n\nTHEN    on a cache miss or a purge, the CDN pulls once from Agent Config Service,\n        which builds the manifest out of the control database.\n\nENDS    the agent adopts the new interval on its next wake-up.\n\nCYCLE   this is the emergency brake for the whole fleet. if a firmware bug makes\n        100 million machines too loud, we change ONE file and they all slow down\n        within the hour  -  no deploy, no per-device action, nothing to roll out.\n        quarantine is the stick you use on one device. this is the one you use on\n        a million.","originalText":"STARTS  the agent boots, or an hour has passed since it last asked.\n\nTHEN    it asks the CDN for its manifest: which datapoints to send, how often, what\n        sampling to apply. 100 M agents asking hourly is ~28 000 requests a second,\n        and not one of them reaches us. the edge answers.\n\nTHEN    on a cache miss or a purge, the CDN pulls once from Agent Config Service,\n        which builds the manifest out of the control database.\n\nENDS    the agent adopts the new interval on its next wake-up.\n\nCYCLE   this is the emergency brake for the whole fleet. if a firmware bug makes\n        100 million machines too loud, we change ONE file and they all slow down\n        within the hour  -  no deploy, no per-device action, nothing to roll out.\n        quarantine is the stick you use on one device. this is the one you use on\n        a million.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsf"},{"id":"_hcGzX06aWkr44MdZ9-qb","type":"rectangle","x":-3287.166929001238,"y":5771.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":67386143,"version":106,"versionNonce":1506011281,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsg"},{"id":"U1c46gxloTZ5H7L-o8fB8","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":5787.661547761547,"width":140.4,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1596817215,"version":106,"versionNonce":54093055,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"3.  THE AUDIT FLOW","originalText":"3.  THE AUDIT FLOW","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsh"},{"id":"h41TaR3krvcM4OpFSQSUu","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":5819.661547761547,"width":469.04,"height":247.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1759067999,"version":106,"versionNonce":1933225585,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  Raw Archiver reads telemetry.raw in its own consumer group.\n\nTHEN    it buffers into big columnar files  -  never many small ones, because the\n        small-file problem is what actually kills a data lake  -  compresses with\n        zstd and writes one object per hour per tenant into the audit lake, under\n        object lock, so nobody including us can edit history.\n\nTHEN    it writes one manifest row per object: key, tenant, min and max timestamp,\n        row count, checksum.\n\nENDS    an hourly compaction job merges the small files and drops duplicates by\n        event id.\n\nCYCLE   when an auditor asks for a date range, Audit Export Service reads the\n        MANIFEST, never the lake, works out exactly which objects are involved and\n        hands back a signed link. the bytes never pass through our services.\n        this group is allowed to be thirty minutes behind and nobody notices  -\n        which is why it is the first thing we pause when the cluster is short.","originalText":"STARTS  Raw Archiver reads telemetry.raw in its own consumer group.\n\nTHEN    it buffers into big columnar files  -  never many small ones, because the\n        small-file problem is what actually kills a data lake  -  compresses with\n        zstd and writes one object per hour per tenant into the audit lake, under\n        object lock, so nobody including us can edit history.\n\nTHEN    it writes one manifest row per object: key, tenant, min and max timestamp,\n        row count, checksum.\n\nENDS    an hourly compaction job merges the small files and drops duplicates by\n        event id.\n\nCYCLE   when an auditor asks for a date range, Audit Export Service reads the\n        MANIFEST, never the lake, works out exactly which objects are involved and\n        hands back a signed link. the bytes never pass through our services.\n        this group is allowed to be thirty minutes behind and nobody notices  -\n        which is why it is the first thing we pause when the cluster is short.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsi"},{"id":"KcY_vCngDi8gZg6aStie4","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":6191.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":592968575,"version":106,"versionNonce":1977773343,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsj"},{"id":"9JbMbOPDEhEKmTgPq0xPz","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":6207.661547761547,"width":413.40000000000003,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1927951263,"version":106,"versionNonce":563984465,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"4.  THE STATE FLOW  -  the narrow point of the system","originalText":"4.  THE STATE FLOW  -  the narrow point of the system","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsk"},{"id":"Y1o1cGms2WsbQeEkHAFJe","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":6239.661547761547,"width":480.48,"height":247.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1606444991,"version":106,"versionNonce":443904319,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  State Materializer reads the same telemetry.raw records.\n\nTHEN    for each device it compares the incoming sequence number with the one it\n        holds. older or equal is dropped on the spot  -  that single comparison is\n        both the duplicate defence and the out-of-order defence.\n\nTHEN    it overwrites that device's latest state, appends the reading to the\n        device's own partition in Cassandra, and works out which BUCKET the reading\n        falls into:  critical under 10 %,  low 10 to 30,  healthy above 30.\n\nENDS    if the bucket is the same as last time it writes NOTHING further.\n        if it changed, and only then, it produces one small record onto\n        device.state.\n\nCYCLE   this is the narrow point of the whole system. 3.3 M readings a second go in;\n        about 40 000 bucket changes a second come out, because only 1 to 2 % of\n        readings actually move a device between buckets. everything downstream of\n        here is roughly eighty times cheaper than everything upstream of it.","originalText":"STARTS  State Materializer reads the same telemetry.raw records.\n\nTHEN    for each device it compares the incoming sequence number with the one it\n        holds. older or equal is dropped on the spot  -  that single comparison is\n        both the duplicate defence and the out-of-order defence.\n\nTHEN    it overwrites that device's latest state, appends the reading to the\n        device's own partition in Cassandra, and works out which BUCKET the reading\n        falls into:  critical under 10 %,  low 10 to 30,  healthy above 30.\n\nENDS    if the bucket is the same as last time it writes NOTHING further.\n        if it changed, and only then, it produces one small record onto\n        device.state.\n\nCYCLE   this is the narrow point of the whole system. 3.3 M readings a second go in;\n        about 40 000 bucket changes a second come out, because only 1 to 2 % of\n        readings actually move a device between buckets. everything downstream of\n        here is roughly eighty times cheaper than everything upstream of it.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsl"},{"id":"wyXD2KHtDTmY6Uwutcn6X","type":"rectangle","x":-4177.166929001238,"y":6191.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":402331615,"version":106,"versionNonce":113542705,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsm"},{"id":"KSINphrMfhsvQgiuSO1_q","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":6207.661547761547,"width":475.8,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":833274879,"version":106,"versionNonce":441643359,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"5.  THE COUNTING FLOW  -  and the repair that keeps it honest","originalText":"5.  THE COUNTING FLOW  -  and the repair that keeps it honest","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsn"},{"id":"gq6et-HZ2a_bvj8Rp7csL","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":6239.661547761547,"width":480.48,"height":233.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":751823903,"version":106,"versionNonce":496408593,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  Rollup Job reads device.state  -  the bucket changes, not the firehose.\n\nTHEN    for every change it moves exactly two numbers: one down in the old bucket,\n        one up in the new one, inside that tenant's counter hash. that hash is a few\n        hundred numbers, under 4 KB, and it is the ENTIRE answer to the question\n        '20 % of devices are battery depleted'.\n\nTHEN    in parallel it folds the same change into a per-minute cube keyed by tenant,\n        minute, model, OS version, firmware and region  -  so every slice an admin\n        might ask for has already been counted before they ask for it.\n\nENDS    every five minutes a reconciliation pass recomputes the exact totals from\n        the cube and OVERWRITES the counters.\n\nCYCLE   that last step is what makes approximate counters safe to build on. a worker\n        can die mid-flight and lose a decrement; the error is real, but it is\n        bounded, and it is never older than five minutes.","originalText":"STARTS  Rollup Job reads device.state  -  the bucket changes, not the firehose.\n\nTHEN    for every change it moves exactly two numbers: one down in the old bucket,\n        one up in the new one, inside that tenant's counter hash. that hash is a few\n        hundred numbers, under 4 KB, and it is the ENTIRE answer to the question\n        '20 % of devices are battery depleted'.\n\nTHEN    in parallel it folds the same change into a per-minute cube keyed by tenant,\n        minute, model, OS version, firmware and region  -  so every slice an admin\n        might ask for has already been counted before they ask for it.\n\nENDS    every five minutes a reconciliation pass recomputes the exact totals from\n        the cube and OVERWRITES the counters.\n\nCYCLE   that last step is what makes approximate counters safe to build on. a worker\n        can die mid-flight and lose a decrement; the error is real, but it is\n        bounded, and it is never older than five minutes.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bso"},{"id":"NdhxPGb9sIT_A88ncuj7-","type":"rectangle","x":-3287.166929001238,"y":6191.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":895160383,"version":106,"versionNonce":516648319,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsp"},{"id":"miJ-QBYgdTf4cYDLLY-U0","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":6207.661547761547,"width":343.2,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":623685727,"version":106,"versionNonce":403402225,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"6.  THE DASHBOARD FLOW  -  the snapshot half","originalText":"6.  THE DASHBOARD FLOW  -  the snapshot half","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsq"},{"id":"JucFgvob66QRDu8RMdhhv","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":6239.661547761547,"width":469.04,"height":220,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1175800959,"version":106,"versionNonce":1394566559,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  an admin opens the fleet screen.\n\nTHEN    ONE request to Query Service: give me the summary. it reads the counter\n        hash  -  one call, about a millisecond  -  and returns the totals with an\n        asOf sequence number stamped on them.\n\nTHEN    when the admin slices it by model or OS version, that is a different read:\n        one query against the cube, 50 to 100 ms, still no raw event involved.\n        when they click a number, that is a third kind of read: fifty devices in\n        that bucket, with a cursor, out of the latest-state store.\n\nENDS    about 115 ms at p95, and the screen shows the asOf time beside the number.\n\nCYCLE   the admin never asks for the summary again. from this point on it is push,\n        and the sequence number they were handed is the cursor that makes the push\n        resumable. snapshot first, then delta, always in that order.","originalText":"STARTS  an admin opens the fleet screen.\n\nTHEN    ONE request to Query Service: give me the summary. it reads the counter\n        hash  -  one call, about a millisecond  -  and returns the totals with an\n        asOf sequence number stamped on them.\n\nTHEN    when the admin slices it by model or OS version, that is a different read:\n        one query against the cube, 50 to 100 ms, still no raw event involved.\n        when they click a number, that is a third kind of read: fifty devices in\n        that bucket, with a cursor, out of the latest-state store.\n\nENDS    about 115 ms at p95, and the screen shows the asOf time beside the number.\n\nCYCLE   the admin never asks for the summary again. from this point on it is push,\n        and the sequence number they were handed is the cursor that makes the push\n        resumable. snapshot first, then delta, always in that order.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsr"},{"id":"fwwTD2NYUDVTDPuqY9H5K","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":6611.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1406493855,"version":106,"versionNonce":2099939281,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bss"},{"id":"o9aTtEL1G1HLLeFI1YxJU","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":6627.661547761547,"width":319.8,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1042118847,"version":106,"versionNonce":645880255,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"7.  THE LIVE TILE FLOW  -  the delta half","originalText":"7.  THE LIVE TILE FLOW  -  the delta half","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bst"},{"id":"lpncEf7V9lLrqiPBq5b2W","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":6659.661547761547,"width":480.48,"height":247.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":713951455,"version":106,"versionNonce":118187441,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  the browser opens a socket and sends the sequence number it was given with\n        the summary.\n\nTHEN    the WebSocket Gateway reads the replay log forward from that sequence, sends\n        everything the browser missed, and only THEN switches it to live.\n\nTHEN    Tile Publisher, meanwhile, turns each counter change into one small update,\n        stamps it with the next sequence number, and appends it to the replay log,\n        which keeps the last five minutes capped by LENGTH, not by hope.\n\nENDS    each gateway node subscribes to a tenant's stream ONCE and writes to every\n        socket it holds. never one subscription per browser.\n\nCYCLE   if a node dies, the browser notices within two missed pings (15 s apart),\n        waits a fully jittered backoff so that 50 000 dashboards do not all come\n        back in the same second, reconnects to ANY other node and replays from its\n        cursor. if the gap is bigger than the log, the server says 'resync' and the\n        browser drops back to flow 6. no branch of this ends with a wrong number.","originalText":"STARTS  the browser opens a socket and sends the sequence number it was given with\n        the summary.\n\nTHEN    the WebSocket Gateway reads the replay log forward from that sequence, sends\n        everything the browser missed, and only THEN switches it to live.\n\nTHEN    Tile Publisher, meanwhile, turns each counter change into one small update,\n        stamps it with the next sequence number, and appends it to the replay log,\n        which keeps the last five minutes capped by LENGTH, not by hope.\n\nENDS    each gateway node subscribes to a tenant's stream ONCE and writes to every\n        socket it holds. never one subscription per browser.\n\nCYCLE   if a node dies, the browser notices within two missed pings (15 s apart),\n        waits a fully jittered backoff so that 50 000 dashboards do not all come\n        back in the same second, reconnects to ANY other node and replays from its\n        cursor. if the gap is bigger than the log, the server says 'resync' and the\n        browser drops back to flow 6. no branch of this ends with a wrong number.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsu"},{"id":"5B8sp2uuv_P3tfNQdCExa","type":"rectangle","x":-4177.166929001238,"y":6611.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":918000895,"version":106,"versionNonce":1309395423,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsv"},{"id":"3L6bTcKn9LnMZ9-p6l6tV","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":6627.661547761547,"width":140.4,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":2048564511,"version":106,"versionNonce":1087735697,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"8.  THE ALERT FLOW","originalText":"8.  THE ALERT FLOW","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsw"},{"id":"-Oc8m6bvL1EHDNH7qG2At","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":6659.661547761547,"width":486.2,"height":247.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1210556735,"version":106,"versionNonce":114636287,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  Alert Engine reads device.state and the rollup windows, holding every rule\n        an admin has written.\n\nTHEN    a rule that becomes true does NOT fire. it moves to PENDING and has to stay\n        true for its whole 'for' duration  -  three windows  -  before it fires.\n        that single delay is what stops a fleet-wide flap from paging anybody.\n\nTHEN    when it does fire, the engine writes the state change into its own Postgres,\n        keyed by a FINGERPRINT of the rule plus the group, not by an event. a second\n        firing of the same fingerprint updates that row instead of creating another.\n\nENDS    Notifier picks it up and sends ONE message: '20 % of fleet battery critical,\n        203 441 devices', with a link to the drill-down. not 203 441 messages.\n\nCYCLE   inhibition suppresses the children of a bigger alert  -  if a region is down,\n        the fifty thousand 'device silent' alerts from that region never leave the\n        building. it resolves the way it fired: three quiet windows, one resolution\n        message, the same row updated once more.","originalText":"STARTS  Alert Engine reads device.state and the rollup windows, holding every rule\n        an admin has written.\n\nTHEN    a rule that becomes true does NOT fire. it moves to PENDING and has to stay\n        true for its whole 'for' duration  -  three windows  -  before it fires.\n        that single delay is what stops a fleet-wide flap from paging anybody.\n\nTHEN    when it does fire, the engine writes the state change into its own Postgres,\n        keyed by a FINGERPRINT of the rule plus the group, not by an event. a second\n        firing of the same fingerprint updates that row instead of creating another.\n\nENDS    Notifier picks it up and sends ONE message: '20 % of fleet battery critical,\n        203 441 devices', with a link to the drill-down. not 203 441 messages.\n\nCYCLE   inhibition suppresses the children of a bigger alert  -  if a region is down,\n        the fifty thousand 'device silent' alerts from that region never leave the\n        building. it resolves the way it fired: three quiet windows, one resolution\n        message, the same row updated once more.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsx"},{"id":"oru6rf6ryhEBQ2v32fBc0","type":"rectangle","x":-3287.166929001238,"y":6611.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":842998111,"version":106,"versionNonce":1690332529,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bsy"},{"id":"vS5QHSapGe_oMzDF6Uo_d","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":6627.661547761547,"width":358.8,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1236376959,"version":106,"versionNonce":400301599,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"9.  THE SILENCE FLOW  -  the one people forget","originalText":"9.  THE SILENCE FLOW  -  the one people forget","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bsz"},{"id":"PzEiaKYIUuDBHYhVTT-ZC","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":6659.661547761547,"width":480.48,"height":206.25,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":566203807,"version":106,"versionNonce":2048104273,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  every accepted reading also drops the device's id into a time slot in the\n        heartbeat wheel: the slot for the current 30 seconds.\n\nTHEN    Presence Sweeper walks slots older than 90 seconds  -  three missed pushes\n        -  looking for devices that appear in an old slot and nowhere newer.\n\nENDS    each one it finds produces a device.silent record, which then flows through\n        exactly the same pipeline as a real reading: it moves that device into the\n        SILENT bucket, which moves the counters, which can trigger an alert.\n\nCYCLE   forgetting this flow is precisely how '20 % depleted' quietly becomes a lie.\n        without it, dead devices stay in the healthy bucket forever and every\n        percentage is computed against a fleet that no longer exists.\n        the general rule: silence has to be turned into an EVENT before anything in\n        an event-driven system can do anything about it. absence is not a signal.","originalText":"STARTS  every accepted reading also drops the device's id into a time slot in the\n        heartbeat wheel: the slot for the current 30 seconds.\n\nTHEN    Presence Sweeper walks slots older than 90 seconds  -  three missed pushes\n        -  looking for devices that appear in an old slot and nowhere newer.\n\nENDS    each one it finds produces a device.silent record, which then flows through\n        exactly the same pipeline as a real reading: it moves that device into the\n        SILENT bucket, which moves the counters, which can trigger an alert.\n\nCYCLE   forgetting this flow is precisely how '20 % depleted' quietly becomes a lie.\n        without it, dead devices stay in the healthy bucket forever and every\n        percentage is computed against a fleet that no longer exists.\n        the general rule: silence has to be turned into an EVENT before anything in\n        an event-driven system can do anything about it. absence is not a signal.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt0"},{"id":"ybsLAwxty0qeyBtNJ-OK0","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":7031.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":295274943,"version":106,"versionNonce":336659007,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bt1"},{"id":"ISaWMynkBKspwpr4QYhX7","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":7047.661547761547,"width":249.60000000000002,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":912259551,"version":106,"versionNonce":858162481,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"10.  THE FAILURE AND REPLAY FLOW","originalText":"10.  THE FAILURE AND REPLAY FLOW","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt2"},{"id":"WTe8jWKa_ETSArbKhHeST","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":7079.661547761547,"width":480.48,"height":247.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":891512319,"version":106,"versionNonce":1549034079,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  a consumer hits a payload it cannot handle  -  a schema it does not know, a\n        field that will not parse.\n\nTHEN    it tries three times, then writes the payload, its offset and the reason\n        into the dead letter store, commits the offset, and moves on. a partition is\n        NEVER allowed to stop because of one bad record.\n\nTHEN    a human looks at what has accumulated, fixes the cause (usually a schema\n        change), and DLQ Replayer produces the fixed records back onto the topic.\n\nENDS    they flow through normally. nothing is ever quietly thrown away.\n\nCYCLE   the same machinery covers a much worse day. if a group falls hours behind,\n        everything it missed is still in Kafka for seven days. if it cannot catch up\n        in time, we seek the dashboard group to the HEAD of the topic  -  the screen\n        becomes right about NOW immediately  -  and let the five minute\n        reconciliation repair the counters from the cube. correctness comes back on\n        its own. a live view does not.","originalText":"STARTS  a consumer hits a payload it cannot handle  -  a schema it does not know, a\n        field that will not parse.\n\nTHEN    it tries three times, then writes the payload, its offset and the reason\n        into the dead letter store, commits the offset, and moves on. a partition is\n        NEVER allowed to stop because of one bad record.\n\nTHEN    a human looks at what has accumulated, fixes the cause (usually a schema\n        change), and DLQ Replayer produces the fixed records back onto the topic.\n\nENDS    they flow through normally. nothing is ever quietly thrown away.\n\nCYCLE   the same machinery covers a much worse day. if a group falls hours behind,\n        everything it missed is still in Kafka for seven days. if it cannot catch up\n        in time, we seek the dashboard group to the HEAD of the topic  -  the screen\n        becomes right about NOW immediately  -  and let the five minute\n        reconciliation repair the counters from the cube. correctness comes back on\n        its own. a live view does not.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt3"},{"id":"M9OJScdbjgrodF8KpPPXg","type":"rectangle","x":-4177.166929001238,"y":7031.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1021595167,"version":106,"versionNonce":190887697,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bt4"},{"id":"tqb1B5PV0a41TCQFXYqsU","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":7047.661547761547,"width":163.8,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1217606207,"version":106,"versionNonce":1635171967,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"11.  THE CONTROL FLOW","originalText":"11.  THE CONTROL FLOW","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt5"},{"id":"cmH0Tm8XsWcnOZAldBsRp","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":7079.661547761547,"width":480.48,"height":220,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":554734175,"version":106,"versionNonce":624536817,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"STARTS  an operator provisions a device, or an admin writes an alerting rule, or\n        somebody changes who is allowed to look at which fleet.\n\nTHEN    the admin gateway authenticates a HUMAN  -  a completely different path from\n        the device one, with different credentials and a different rate limit  -\n        and Control Plane Service writes it to the control database. this is the\n        only transactional write anywhere in the diagram, and it is tiny.\n\nENDS    the change is picked up by whoever needs it: the alert engine reloads its\n        rules, the guard rail refreshes its device-to-tenant map, the manifest is\n        rebuilt and pushed to the edge.\n\nCYCLE   this whole band sits deliberately outside the telemetry pipeline. it is\n        small, it is strongly consistent, and it is allowed to be briefly\n        unavailable  -  because nothing in the ingest path ever blocks on it.\n        a device can keep reporting perfectly well while the admin site is down.","originalText":"STARTS  an operator provisions a device, or an admin writes an alerting rule, or\n        somebody changes who is allowed to look at which fleet.\n\nTHEN    the admin gateway authenticates a HUMAN  -  a completely different path from\n        the device one, with different credentials and a different rate limit  -\n        and Control Plane Service writes it to the control database. this is the\n        only transactional write anywhere in the diagram, and it is tiny.\n\nENDS    the change is picked up by whoever needs it: the alert engine reloads its\n        rules, the guard rail refreshes its device-to-tenant map, the manifest is\n        rebuilt and pushed to the edge.\n\nCYCLE   this whole band sits deliberately outside the telemetry pipeline. it is\n        small, it is strongly consistent, and it is allowed to be briefly\n        unavailable  -  because nothing in the ingest path ever blocks on it.\n        a device can keep reporting perfectly well while the admin site is down.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt6"},{"id":"sGN2M8F46mSCI74ciZsQy","type":"rectangle","x":-3287.166929001238,"y":7031.661547761547,"width":860,"height":390,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":523038335,"version":106,"versionNonce":641242783,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"bt7"},{"id":"y8QjUrwbc4TIgUKKW2jeV","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":7047.661547761547,"width":429,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":766035615,"version":106,"versionNonce":1748559569,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE 20 SECOND BUDGET  -  and why it is loose on purpose","originalText":"THE 20 SECOND BUDGET  -  and why it is loose on purpose","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt8"},{"id":"V5hqMa9GK0iEhQPox7ktu","type":"text","x":-3267.166929001238,"y":7079.661547761547,"width":446.16,"height":220,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":187996863,"version":106,"versionNonce":21654207,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"device  ->  edge     TLS and the hop to the region       ~     80 ms\ngateway              the three checks, then to Kafka     ~     30 ms\nkafka                written and acknowledged            ~     20 ms\nconsumer             picked up and processed  ( p99 )    ~    300 ms\nrollup window        a 5 second window has to close      ~  5 000 ms\ncounter + push       written, then sent down the socket  ~     50 ms\n                                                           -----------\n                                                           ~    5.5 s   p99\n\nthe promise                                                     20 s\nheadroom for lag                                           ~   14.5 s\n\nwhich is the whole point of quoting it. the budget is not tight, it is\ndeliberately LOOSE  -  so that a consumer can fall behind by ten seconds, or a\ndeploy can restart a worker, without anybody breaking a promise to anybody.\na design whose latency budget is exactly met on a good day has no good days.","originalText":"device  ->  edge     TLS and the hop to the region       ~     80 ms\ngateway              the three checks, then to Kafka     ~     30 ms\nkafka                written and acknowledged            ~     20 ms\nconsumer             picked up and processed  ( p99 )    ~    300 ms\nrollup window        a 5 second window has to close      ~  5 000 ms\ncounter + push       written, then sent down the socket  ~     50 ms\n                                                           -----------\n                                                           ~    5.5 s   p99\n\nthe promise                                                     20 s\nheadroom for lag                                           ~   14.5 s\n\nwhich is the whole point of quoting it. the budget is not tight, it is\ndeliberately LOOSE  -  so that a consumer can fall behind by ten seconds, or a\ndeploy can restart a worker, without anybody breaking a promise to anybody.\na design whose latency budget is exactly met on a good day has no good days.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bt9"},{"id":"ZqXkAb0PYvAbkP-glf90A","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":7471.661547761547,"width":860,"height":320,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1282468575,"version":106,"versionNonce":1593173169,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btA"},{"id":"EeHh-CkTv6_aulfFILdNR","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":7487.661547761547,"width":202.8,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":502150911,"version":106,"versionNonce":525122271,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE 200 MILLISECOND BUDGET","originalText":"THE 200 MILLISECOND BUDGET","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btB"},{"id":"jE2vfwYItILsXFhV5TvEy","type":"text","x":-5045.166929001238,"y":7523.661547761547,"width":406.12,"height":178.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1177354015,"version":106,"versionNonce":1427646097,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"tls + load balancer                                   ~     20 ms\ntoken check       signed, so no database is touched   ~      2 ms\nthe headline tile one read of one small hash          ~      2 ms\nany other slice   one read of the cube  ( p95 )       ~     60 ms\njson + the wire                                       ~     30 ms\n                                                        -----------\n                                                        ~   115 ms  p95\nthe promise                                                 200 ms\n\nnotice what is NOT on this list. no raw events. no scan. no join. no\nfan-out across devices. if any one of those ever appears in the read\npath the budget is gone, and no amount of caching brings it back  -\nbecause the cache itself would have to be recomputed by a scan.","originalText":"tls + load balancer                                   ~     20 ms\ntoken check       signed, so no database is touched   ~      2 ms\nthe headline tile one read of one small hash          ~      2 ms\nany other slice   one read of the cube  ( p95 )       ~     60 ms\njson + the wire                                       ~     30 ms\n                                                        -----------\n                                                        ~   115 ms  p95\nthe promise                                                 200 ms\n\nnotice what is NOT on this list. no raw events. no scan. no join. no\nfan-out across devices. if any one of those ever appears in the read\npath the budget is gone, and no amount of caching brings it back  -\nbecause the cache itself would have to be recomputed by a scan.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btC"},{"id":"wObexFJn6XK9OxCw49B-M","type":"rectangle","x":-4177.166929001238,"y":7471.661547761547,"width":1770,"height":320,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1738392383,"version":106,"versionNonce":1154483967,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btD"},{"id":"G18txoitfBDeKAdvQMC9j","type":"text","x":-4157.166929001238,"y":7487.661547761547,"width":717.6,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":44106591,"version":106,"versionNonce":894905457,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHY THIS IS NOT A SOCIAL FEED  -  say it out loud, because every feed instinct is wrong here","originalText":"WHY THIS IS NOT A SOCIAL FEED  -  say it out loud, because every feed instinct is wrong here","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btE"},{"id":"c10UxGnqURJ4fOp6C3_Ym","type":"text","x":-4155.166929001238,"y":7527.661547761547,"width":594.88,"height":192.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":931610495,"version":106,"versionNonce":1347735327,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"                                a social feed                      this system\n   traffic shape                100 reads : 1 write                3 300 writes : 1 read\n   the expensive side           the read                           the write\n   the trick                    pre-build every feed               pre-count the whole fleet\n   what gets cached             one entry per user                 a few hundred numbers per tenant\n   who is watching              500 M people                       5 000 admins\n   what must never be lost      the post                           the raw payload\n   what may be stale            the feed, by seconds               the count, by seconds\n   the frightening event        a celebrity posts                  a whole fleet reboots at once\n\nthe read side here is almost free  -  a few thousand admins asking for a few hundred numbers. ALL of the\nengineering is on the write side: 3.3 M events a second arriving from machines that retry forever and\ncannot be patched quickly. that is why there is a queue in the middle, why nothing at all is computed at\nread time, and why every limit answers politely with a retry time instead of failing.","originalText":"                                a social feed                      this system\n   traffic shape                100 reads : 1 write                3 300 writes : 1 read\n   the expensive side           the read                           the write\n   the trick                    pre-build every feed               pre-count the whole fleet\n   what gets cached             one entry per user                 a few hundred numbers per tenant\n   who is watching              500 M people                       5 000 admins\n   what must never be lost      the post                           the raw payload\n   what may be stale            the feed, by seconds               the count, by seconds\n   the frightening event        a celebrity posts                  a whole fleet reboots at once\n\nthe read side here is almost free  -  a few thousand admins asking for a few hundred numbers. ALL of the\nengineering is on the write side: 3.3 M events a second arriving from machines that retry forever and\ncannot be patched quickly. that is why there is a queue in the middle, why nothing at all is computed at\nread time, and why every limit answers politely with a retry time instead of failing.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btF"},{"id":"3hWP0U3hKC55RGBLDH7ws","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":7841.661547761547,"width":516,"height":780,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1217460127,"version":106,"versionNonce":662337105,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btG"},{"id":"qA4BAnlPdS8HTmRjWnqrw","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":7857.661547761547,"width":405.6,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1542368191,"version":106,"versionNonce":2085327679,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"HARD 1  -  a device goes mad: 10 000 events a second","originalText":"HARD 1  -  a device goes mad: 10 000 events a second","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btH"},{"id":"5vc1OqG6XcGeyjDn6tknW","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":7893.661547761547,"width":377.52000000000004,"height":577.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":2109791199,"version":106,"versionNonce":1851360305,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"you cannot trust the agent. it is software you shipped, it is in\nthe field, and you cannot patch it quickly. so the defence is\nlayered, and each layer is cheaper than the one behind it.\n\n1  AT THE EDGE, BEFORE ANYTHING IS PARSED\n   a quarantine list is checked first. an abusing device is\n   rejected in microseconds  -  before the body is read, before\n   any JSON is parsed, before Kafka is touched. one lookup.\n\n2  A TOKEN BUCKET PER DEVICE\n   100 pushes a minute, burst 200. over the line gets 429 with a\n   Retry-After. NEVER a 500, and never a silent drop  -  a 500\n   makes the agent retry immediately, and silence makes it retry\n   forever. the status code IS the rate limiter.\n\n3  THE CHECK ITSELF MUST BE CHEAP\n   at 3.3 M a second you cannot ask Redis about every push. each\n   gateway node holds its own slice of the budget and reconciles\n   once a second. devices hash to nodes, so one device's pushes\n   land on the same node and the local count is very nearly exact.\n\n4  A QUOTA ABOVE THE DEVICE\n   a per-tenant quota, so one customer's broken fleet cannot eat\n   the cluster the others paid for. and a broker-level byte quota\n   under that, so Kafka throttles us even if everything above it\n   has failed.\n\n5  THE BLAST RADIUS IS ALREADY ONE PARTITION\n   the key is the device id, so a mad device jams 1 partition of\n   5 000. if it ever grows big enough to matter, re-key that one\n   device to a random shard and give up its ordering  -  which\n   costs nothing, because every event carries its own sequence.\n\n6  SHED BY PRIORITY, NEVER EVENLY\n   if produce latency climbs, drop heartbeats first and keep\n   crash reports and alerts. uniform shedding throws away the\n   rare events, which are the ones that mattered.\n\n7  AND THEN FIX IT AT THE SOURCE\n   the 202 carries 'next push after N seconds', and the manifest\n   can slow the whole fleet. quarantine is the stick. the\n   manifest is the actual fix.","originalText":"you cannot trust the agent. it is software you shipped, it is in\nthe field, and you cannot patch it quickly. so the defence is\nlayered, and each layer is cheaper than the one behind it.\n\n1  AT THE EDGE, BEFORE ANYTHING IS PARSED\n   a quarantine list is checked first. an abusing device is\n   rejected in microseconds  -  before the body is read, before\n   any JSON is parsed, before Kafka is touched. one lookup.\n\n2  A TOKEN BUCKET PER DEVICE\n   100 pushes a minute, burst 200. over the line gets 429 with a\n   Retry-After. NEVER a 500, and never a silent drop  -  a 500\n   makes the agent retry immediately, and silence makes it retry\n   forever. the status code IS the rate limiter.\n\n3  THE CHECK ITSELF MUST BE CHEAP\n   at 3.3 M a second you cannot ask Redis about every push. each\n   gateway node holds its own slice of the budget and reconciles\n   once a second. devices hash to nodes, so one device's pushes\n   land on the same node and the local count is very nearly exact.\n\n4  A QUOTA ABOVE THE DEVICE\n   a per-tenant quota, so one customer's broken fleet cannot eat\n   the cluster the others paid for. and a broker-level byte quota\n   under that, so Kafka throttles us even if everything above it\n   has failed.\n\n5  THE BLAST RADIUS IS ALREADY ONE PARTITION\n   the key is the device id, so a mad device jams 1 partition of\n   5 000. if it ever grows big enough to matter, re-key that one\n   device to a random shard and give up its ordering  -  which\n   costs nothing, because every event carries its own sequence.\n\n6  SHED BY PRIORITY, NEVER EVENLY\n   if produce latency climbs, drop heartbeats first and keep\n   crash reports and alerts. uniform shedding throws away the\n   rare events, which are the ones that mattered.\n\n7  AND THEN FIX IT AT THE SOURCE\n   the 202 carries 'next push after N seconds', and the manifest\n   can slow the whole fleet. quarantine is the stick. the\n   manifest is the actual fix.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btI"},{"id":"DHCh4WBQam19UvingbRRX","type":"rectangle","x":-4531.166929001238,"y":7841.661547761547,"width":516,"height":780,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1645742079,"version":106,"versionNonce":1628754783,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btJ"},{"id":"QMQK5C0vw33VbIt6NLd8n","type":"text","x":-4511.166929001238,"y":7857.661547761547,"width":429,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1727311903,"version":106,"versionNonce":98293265,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"HARD 2  -  '20 % depleted' over 500 k devices in 200 ms","originalText":"HARD 2  -  '20 % depleted' over 500 k devices in 200 ms","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btK"},{"id":"2K5sLSvRQMHQreprRLx_x","type":"text","x":-4511.166929001238,"y":7893.661547761547,"width":377.52000000000004,"height":563.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1447515199,"version":106,"versionNonce":922816383,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"the wrong answer is to scan 500 k rows when the admin looks. the\nright answer is that the number was already finished BEFORE they\nlooked at it.\n\nCOUNT ON WRITE, NOT ON READ\nevery reading falls into a bucket  -  critical under 10 %, low 10\nto 30, healthy above 30. the materializer remembers which bucket\neach device was in LAST time.\n\n    same bucket as last time   ->   write nothing at all\n    a different bucket         ->   one down, one up\n\nonly 1 to 2 % of snapshots actually change bucket, so 3.3 M\nreadings a second become roughly 40 000 counter moves a second.\nthat single fact is the entire trick.\n\nTHE READ IS THEN ONE CALL\nthe tile is one small hash per tenant  -  a handful of numbers.\n'20 % depleted' is critical divided by total. about 1 ms.\n\nANY OTHER SLICE THEY ASK FOR\nby model, by OS version, by region  -  the full cross product is\nfar too big to hold in Redis. so the rollup job ALSO writes a\nper-minute cube into a column store, which answers a 500 k row\nslice in 50 to 100 ms with no index at all.\n\nTWO THINGS THAT WOULD OTHERWISE ROT IT\n  DRIFT    counters miss decrements when a worker dies mid-flight.\n           so every 5 minutes the exact count from the cube\n           OVERWRITES them. fast reads, bounded error, and the\n           error is never older than five minutes.\n\n  SILENCE  a device that stops reporting must LEAVE the counters,\n           or '20 %' quietly becomes a lie about a smaller and\n           smaller fleet. the heartbeat wheel moves anything\n           unheard for 90 s into the silent bucket.\n\nAND THE DRILL DOWN IS A DIFFERENT QUERY\nclicking the tile means 'give me 50 devices whose bucket is\ncritical, with a cursor'  -  a keyed, paginated read of the state\nstore. it is never the same query as the tile.","originalText":"the wrong answer is to scan 500 k rows when the admin looks. the\nright answer is that the number was already finished BEFORE they\nlooked at it.\n\nCOUNT ON WRITE, NOT ON READ\nevery reading falls into a bucket  -  critical under 10 %, low 10\nto 30, healthy above 30. the materializer remembers which bucket\neach device was in LAST time.\n\n    same bucket as last time   ->   write nothing at all\n    a different bucket         ->   one down, one up\n\nonly 1 to 2 % of snapshots actually change bucket, so 3.3 M\nreadings a second become roughly 40 000 counter moves a second.\nthat single fact is the entire trick.\n\nTHE READ IS THEN ONE CALL\nthe tile is one small hash per tenant  -  a handful of numbers.\n'20 % depleted' is critical divided by total. about 1 ms.\n\nANY OTHER SLICE THEY ASK FOR\nby model, by OS version, by region  -  the full cross product is\nfar too big to hold in Redis. so the rollup job ALSO writes a\nper-minute cube into a column store, which answers a 500 k row\nslice in 50 to 100 ms with no index at all.\n\nTWO THINGS THAT WOULD OTHERWISE ROT IT\n  DRIFT    counters miss decrements when a worker dies mid-flight.\n           so every 5 minutes the exact count from the cube\n           OVERWRITES them. fast reads, bounded error, and the\n           error is never older than five minutes.\n\n  SILENCE  a device that stops reporting must LEAVE the counters,\n           or '20 %' quietly becomes a lie about a smaller and\n           smaller fleet. the heartbeat wheel moves anything\n           unheard for 90 s into the silent bucket.\n\nAND THE DRILL DOWN IS A DIFFERENT QUERY\nclicking the tile means 'give me 50 devices whose bucket is\ncritical, with a cursor'  -  a keyed, paginated read of the state\nstore. it is never the same query as the tile.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btL"},{"id":"ZNrF_PDjchnBMEvjAWnd_","type":"rectangle","x":-3995.166929001238,"y":7841.661547761547,"width":516,"height":780,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1743811679,"version":106,"versionNonce":16313329,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btM"},{"id":"R2Q8_9TXFeqR6lGOEN1ZJ","type":"text","x":-3975.166929001238,"y":7857.661547761547,"width":343.2,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":62951551,"version":106,"versionNonce":1481688991,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"HARD 3  -  the consumer is 10 minutes behind","originalText":"HARD 3  -  the consumer is 10 minutes behind","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btN"},{"id":"0p2hl5jzOKfA2Y0tktK1Y","type":"text","x":-3975.166929001238,"y":7893.661547761547,"width":377.52000000000004,"height":577.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1131948191,"version":106,"versionNonce":666387921,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"the promise was 20 seconds, so 10 minutes is a broken promise and\nan on-call action, not a shrug.\n\nFIRST, MEASURE IT IN SECONDS, NOT IN MESSAGES\n200 k behind is fine at 1 M/s and fatal at 100/s. lag divided by\nthe consume rate is a number of SECONDS, and seconds is the unit\nthe promise was made in. warn at 60 s, page at 300 s.\n\nTHEN ASK WHICH PARTITION\n   every partition behind    ->   it is capacity\n   one partition behind      ->   a hot key or a poison message\nthat one question splits the whole problem in two.\n\nIF IT IS CAPACITY\nadd consumers, up to the number of partitions. that ceiling is\nexactly why 5 000 partitions were chosen on day one, when it was\nfree to choose. past it you must repartition, and that is a\nmigration, not something you do at 3 am.\n\nIF IT IS A POISON MESSAGE\na consumer must NEVER block a partition on one bad payload.\nthree tries, then it goes to the dead letter topic with its\noffset and the reason, the offset is committed, and the line\nmoves on. a human replays it later, on purpose.\n\nIF IT IS A REBALANCE STORM\na rolling restart that stops the world every time is its own\noutage. cooperative sticky assignment and fixed member ids, so a\nrestart moves a few partitions instead of all of them.\n\nTHE PART PEOPLE MISS  -  NOT ALL LAG IS EQUAL\nthe same event is read by several groups with different promises.\nthe archiver may be 30 minutes behind and nobody notices. the\ndashboard path may not be. so when we are short, PAUSE the\narchiver and give its CPU and network to the state path.\n\nAND THE ESCAPE HATCH\nif the dashboard path truly cannot catch up, skip it to the head\nof the topic. the screen becomes right about NOW immediately, and\nreconciliation repairs the counters. correctness returns on its\nown; a live view does not. and the screen says 'as of 14:02:31'  -\na stale dashboard that admits it is stale is survivable.","originalText":"the promise was 20 seconds, so 10 minutes is a broken promise and\nan on-call action, not a shrug.\n\nFIRST, MEASURE IT IN SECONDS, NOT IN MESSAGES\n200 k behind is fine at 1 M/s and fatal at 100/s. lag divided by\nthe consume rate is a number of SECONDS, and seconds is the unit\nthe promise was made in. warn at 60 s, page at 300 s.\n\nTHEN ASK WHICH PARTITION\n   every partition behind    ->   it is capacity\n   one partition behind      ->   a hot key or a poison message\nthat one question splits the whole problem in two.\n\nIF IT IS CAPACITY\nadd consumers, up to the number of partitions. that ceiling is\nexactly why 5 000 partitions were chosen on day one, when it was\nfree to choose. past it you must repartition, and that is a\nmigration, not something you do at 3 am.\n\nIF IT IS A POISON MESSAGE\na consumer must NEVER block a partition on one bad payload.\nthree tries, then it goes to the dead letter topic with its\noffset and the reason, the offset is committed, and the line\nmoves on. a human replays it later, on purpose.\n\nIF IT IS A REBALANCE STORM\na rolling restart that stops the world every time is its own\noutage. cooperative sticky assignment and fixed member ids, so a\nrestart moves a few partitions instead of all of them.\n\nTHE PART PEOPLE MISS  -  NOT ALL LAG IS EQUAL\nthe same event is read by several groups with different promises.\nthe archiver may be 30 minutes behind and nobody notices. the\ndashboard path may not be. so when we are short, PAUSE the\narchiver and give its CPU and network to the state path.\n\nAND THE ESCAPE HATCH\nif the dashboard path truly cannot catch up, skip it to the head\nof the topic. the screen becomes right about NOW immediately, and\nreconciliation repairs the counters. correctness returns on its\nown; a live view does not. and the screen says 'as of 14:02:31'  -\na stale dashboard that admits it is stale is survivable.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btO"},{"id":"1Ac1WRTa0X3umEnQ7hyBg","type":"rectangle","x":-3459.166929001238,"y":7841.661547761547,"width":516,"height":780,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":885505215,"version":106,"versionNonce":53771199,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btP"},{"id":"xmDXUjFwF_zC0ukbzyF7K","type":"text","x":-3439.166929001238,"y":7857.661547761547,"width":304.2,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1531956447,"version":106,"versionNonce":1429300145,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"HARD 4  -  the same event arrives twice","originalText":"HARD 4  -  the same event arrives twice","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btQ"},{"id":"Jcf_mT-Hv8KFdrxYvjqid","type":"text","x":-3439.166929001238,"y":7893.661547761547,"width":377.52000000000004,"height":605,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1923143935,"version":106,"versionNonce":1540139999,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"it is not an edge case, it is the normal case. the agent times out\non a request we actually completed and retries. a producer\nretries. a consumer dies after doing the work and before\ncommitting its offset. plan for it everywhere, not in one place.\n\nTHE KEY IS MADE BY THE DEVICE, NOT BY US\nthe agent stamps every snapshot with an event id and a sequence\nnumber when it is CREATED, and reuses both on every retry. an id\nwe generated on arrival would be different each time and would\nprove nothing at all.\n\nSIX PLACES, SIX DIFFERENT ANSWERS\n\n  gateway      a ten minute memory of event ids. a repeat gets\n               the SAME 202 the first one got, so the agent stops\n               retrying and goes away happy. best effort only  -\n               this cache is allowed to be lost.\n\n  into kafka   idempotent producer and full acknowledgement,\n               which kills the duplicates a producer retry would\n               create inside one session.\n\n  state        newest sequence wins, older or equal is dropped.\n               idempotent AND it fixes out-of-order arrival at\n               the same time, for free.\n\n  counters     the one that matters. counters are NEVER moved by\n               an event, they are moved by a CHANGE OF BUCKET. a\n               replayed event computes the same bucket the device\n               is already in, so there is no change, so nothing\n               moves. replay safety falls out of the design.\n\n  audit lake   duplicates are allowed to LAND, and the hourly\n               compaction removes them by event id. the lake\n               values never losing anything far above never\n               repeating anything.\n\n  alerts       an alert is identified by a fingerprint, not by an\n               event. firing twice updates the same row.\n\nEXACTLY ONCE, WHERE IT IS WORTH PAYING FOR\nthe rollup job runs with transactions and checkpoints, so its\nwrites land exactly once. everywhere else is at-least-once plus\nidempotent writes  -  cheaper, simpler, and just as correct.","originalText":"it is not an edge case, it is the normal case. the agent times out\non a request we actually completed and retries. a producer\nretries. a consumer dies after doing the work and before\ncommitting its offset. plan for it everywhere, not in one place.\n\nTHE KEY IS MADE BY THE DEVICE, NOT BY US\nthe agent stamps every snapshot with an event id and a sequence\nnumber when it is CREATED, and reuses both on every retry. an id\nwe generated on arrival would be different each time and would\nprove nothing at all.\n\nSIX PLACES, SIX DIFFERENT ANSWERS\n\n  gateway      a ten minute memory of event ids. a repeat gets\n               the SAME 202 the first one got, so the agent stops\n               retrying and goes away happy. best effort only  -\n               this cache is allowed to be lost.\n\n  into kafka   idempotent producer and full acknowledgement,\n               which kills the duplicates a producer retry would\n               create inside one session.\n\n  state        newest sequence wins, older or equal is dropped.\n               idempotent AND it fixes out-of-order arrival at\n               the same time, for free.\n\n  counters     the one that matters. counters are NEVER moved by\n               an event, they are moved by a CHANGE OF BUCKET. a\n               replayed event computes the same bucket the device\n               is already in, so there is no change, so nothing\n               moves. replay safety falls out of the design.\n\n  audit lake   duplicates are allowed to LAND, and the hourly\n               compaction removes them by event id. the lake\n               values never losing anything far above never\n               repeating anything.\n\n  alerts       an alert is identified by a fingerprint, not by an\n               event. firing twice updates the same row.\n\nEXACTLY ONCE, WHERE IT IS WORTH PAYING FOR\nthe rollup job runs with transactions and checkpoints, so its\nwrites land exactly once. everywhere else is at-least-once plus\nidempotent writes  -  cheaper, simpler, and just as correct.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btR"},{"id":"Q0KHY4VM6JpRZRZH8Z1Qy","type":"rectangle","x":-2923.166929001238,"y":7841.661547761547,"width":516,"height":780,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1882939679,"version":106,"versionNonce":361481617,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btS"},{"id":"LNssa14emaP3_DLVWe43C","type":"text","x":-2903.166929001238,"y":7857.661547761547,"width":280.8,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1167058239,"version":106,"versionNonce":774494207,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"HARD 5  -  the websocket server dies","originalText":"HARD 5  -  the websocket server dies","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btT"},{"id":"39TXnwWB6d7Ce2tHsa4bo","type":"text","x":-2903.166929001238,"y":7893.661547761547,"width":371.8,"height":577.5,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1644094815,"version":106,"versionNonce":67038065,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE RULE THAT MAKES IT SURVIVABLE\nthe socket is an accelerator, never the source of truth. anything\npushed down it must also be reachable by a plain GET. a browser\nthat loses its socket becomes SLOW, not WRONG. everything below\nis just the mechanics of that one sentence.\n\nSNAPSHOT, THEN DELTA\non open the browser asks for the full summary once and gets an\n'as of' sequence number with it. after that it receives only\nchanges, each carrying the next sequence number.\n\nTHE RECONNECT CARRIES THE CURSOR\nthe browser reconnects to ANY node  -  the nodes hold no state\nworth keeping  -  and says 'I last saw sequence N'. the node\nreads the replay log forward from N, sends what was missed, then\nswitches to live. the last five minutes are kept, capped by\nLENGTH, not by hope. a stream, not pub/sub  -  pub/sub drops\neverything on disconnect, which is the exact failure we are\ndefending against.\n\nIF THE GAP IS TOO BIG\nthe laptop was asleep, or the log rolled past N. the server says\n'resync', the browser fetches the summary again and carries on.\nno branch of this ends with silently wrong numbers on a screen.\n\nNOTICING THE DEATH QUICKLY\nan application level ping every 15 s, because a protocol level\nping does not survive every proxy in the path. two missed pongs\nand the client treats the socket as dead.\n\nTHE PART THAT ACTUALLY BREAKS THINGS\n50 000 dashboards all reconnecting in the same second is a denial\nof service we did to ourselves. exponential backoff with FULL\njitter, base 1 s, capped at 30 s. and on a deploy the node sends\n'going away, come back in a random 0 to 5 seconds' and drains for\n30 s, so a rolling restart never stampedes either.\n\nFAN OUT PER NODE, NOT PER SOCKET\neach node subscribes to a tenant's stream ONCE and writes it to\nevery socket it holds. 50 k sockets is small. 50 k subscriptions\nwould not be. and the cursor lives in the browser  -  the only\nplace in this whole diagram that survives our restarts.","originalText":"THE RULE THAT MAKES IT SURVIVABLE\nthe socket is an accelerator, never the source of truth. anything\npushed down it must also be reachable by a plain GET. a browser\nthat loses its socket becomes SLOW, not WRONG. everything below\nis just the mechanics of that one sentence.\n\nSNAPSHOT, THEN DELTA\non open the browser asks for the full summary once and gets an\n'as of' sequence number with it. after that it receives only\nchanges, each carrying the next sequence number.\n\nTHE RECONNECT CARRIES THE CURSOR\nthe browser reconnects to ANY node  -  the nodes hold no state\nworth keeping  -  and says 'I last saw sequence N'. the node\nreads the replay log forward from N, sends what was missed, then\nswitches to live. the last five minutes are kept, capped by\nLENGTH, not by hope. a stream, not pub/sub  -  pub/sub drops\neverything on disconnect, which is the exact failure we are\ndefending against.\n\nIF THE GAP IS TOO BIG\nthe laptop was asleep, or the log rolled past N. the server says\n'resync', the browser fetches the summary again and carries on.\nno branch of this ends with silently wrong numbers on a screen.\n\nNOTICING THE DEATH QUICKLY\nan application level ping every 15 s, because a protocol level\nping does not survive every proxy in the path. two missed pongs\nand the client treats the socket as dead.\n\nTHE PART THAT ACTUALLY BREAKS THINGS\n50 000 dashboards all reconnecting in the same second is a denial\nof service we did to ourselves. exponential backoff with FULL\njitter, base 1 s, capped at 30 s. and on a deploy the node sends\n'going away, come back in a random 0 to 5 seconds' and drains for\n30 s, so a rolling restart never stampedes either.\n\nFAN OUT PER NODE, NOT PER SOCKET\neach node subscribes to a tenant's stream ONCE and writes it to\nevery socket it holds. 50 k sockets is small. 50 k subscriptions\nwould not be. and the cursor lives in the browser  -  the only\nplace in this whole diagram that survives our restarts.","fontSize":11,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btU"},{"id":"VoM1D5-tiUhgQK25lU-Sf","type":"rectangle","x":-5067.166929001238,"y":8661.661547761547,"width":1310,"height":560,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1960370559,"version":106,"versionNonce":1884061727,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btV"},{"id":"CRIT2mGPsotZFsKLANkbK","type":"text","x":-5047.166929001238,"y":8677.661547761547,"width":195,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1868454303,"version":106,"versionNonce":1758614865,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"RULES THAT MAKE THIS WORK","originalText":"RULES THAT MAKE THIS WORK","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btW"},{"id":"esvq_bJTD8Z0_xf7eVN1b","type":"text","x":-5045.166929001238,"y":8715.661547761547,"width":492.96000000000004,"height":465,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1186975167,"version":106,"versionNonce":69542975,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"1   COUNT ON WRITE, NEVER SCAN ON READ. the number the dashboard shows was\n     finished before anybody asked for it. every budget here comes from that.\n\n2   the ingest path does exactly three things: check it, stamp it, hand it to\n     Kafka. anything else that has to happen belongs to a consumer.\n\n3   nothing downstream may slow an agent down, and no consumer may slow another\n     consumer down. separate groups, separate promises, separate right to lag.\n\n4   a repeat must change nothing. that is a property built into every consumer,\n     not something we hope a cache remembered for us.\n\n5   TWO CLOCKS, KEPT APART. the device's clock decides where a reading sits on\n     ITS chart. our clock decides which window it is counted in. device clocks\n     are wrong, we cannot fix them, and windowing on theirs would make the\n     20 second promise unkeepable.\n\n6   every screen says how old it is. stale is fine. lying is not.\n\n7   a device going quiet is an EVENT, not the absence of one. something has to\n     be actively watching for silence, or the fleet quietly shrinks and every\n     percentage becomes wrong.\n\n8   raw payloads never go near a database. object storage, written once, and\n     the manifest is the thing we actually query.\n\n9   the blast radius of one bad device is one partition out of five thousand.\n\n10  every limit answers with a 429 and a retry time. a 500 makes a fleet of\n     100 million machines retry HARDER, which is how a small problem turns\n     into a real outage.","originalText":"1   COUNT ON WRITE, NEVER SCAN ON READ. the number the dashboard shows was\n     finished before anybody asked for it. every budget here comes from that.\n\n2   the ingest path does exactly three things: check it, stamp it, hand it to\n     Kafka. anything else that has to happen belongs to a consumer.\n\n3   nothing downstream may slow an agent down, and no consumer may slow another\n     consumer down. separate groups, separate promises, separate right to lag.\n\n4   a repeat must change nothing. that is a property built into every consumer,\n     not something we hope a cache remembered for us.\n\n5   TWO CLOCKS, KEPT APART. the device's clock decides where a reading sits on\n     ITS chart. our clock decides which window it is counted in. device clocks\n     are wrong, we cannot fix them, and windowing on theirs would make the\n     20 second promise unkeepable.\n\n6   every screen says how old it is. stale is fine. lying is not.\n\n7   a device going quiet is an EVENT, not the absence of one. something has to\n     be actively watching for silence, or the fleet quietly shrinks and every\n     percentage becomes wrong.\n\n8   raw payloads never go near a database. object storage, written once, and\n     the manifest is the thing we actually query.\n\n9   the blast radius of one bad device is one partition out of five thousand.\n\n10  every limit answers with a 429 and a retry time. a 500 makes a fleet of\n     100 million machines retry HARDER, which is how a small problem turns\n     into a real outage.","fontSize":12,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btX"},{"id":"G2yJnebQX_nD-5E2cDXjP","type":"rectangle","x":-3717.166929001238,"y":8661.661547761547,"width":1310,"height":560,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"dashed","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1981660639,"version":106,"versionNonce":392004401,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"roundness":{"type":3},"index":"btY"},{"id":"ZlfxjlBBlQNaONqDLVrVR","type":"text","x":-3697.166929001238,"y":8677.661547761547,"width":226.20000000000002,"height":18.75,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":1082698239,"version":106,"versionNonce":966018143,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE WHOLE THING IN ONE BREATH","originalText":"THE WHOLE THING IN ONE BREATH","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"btZ"},{"id":"uWTHEEPGKzMKDvlQEwSDA","type":"text","x":-3695.166929001238,"y":8715.661547761547,"width":517.9200000000001,"height":375,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"seed":855708191,"version":106,"versionNonce":201848081,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1787459983943,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WRITE     an agent pushes one snapshot  ->  the gateway checks who it is and\n          whether it is allowed to be this loud  ->  Ingest stamps it with OUR\n          clock and hands it to Kafka  ->  202. the request is over in ~30 ms\n          and nothing downstream of Kafka was ever in its way.\n\nFAN OUT   the same event is then read by several consumers, each with its own\n          promise and its own right to fall behind:\n             ARCHIVER      writes it into the lake, where it cannot be edited\n             MATERIALIZER  keeps the newest reading, shouts ONLY on a bucket change\n             ROLLUP        counts the fleet every 5 s, into counters and a cube\n             ALERT ENGINE  holds a rule 3 windows, then pages once, with a count\n             SWEEPER       watches for silence, because silence is an event too\n\nREAD      the admin opens the screen  ->  Query Service reads a handful of\n          numbers  ->  about 115 ms. any slice they ask for next comes from the\n          cube, never from raw events. the tiles then keep moving because the\n          socket sends deltas, each carrying a sequence number.\n\nREPAIR    every 5 minutes the exact count from the cube overwrites the fast\n          counters, so drift is never older than 5 minutes. anything a consumer\n          choked on waits in the dead letter store until a human replays it.\n          anything a consumer missed is still in Kafka for 7 days.\n\n          nothing here has to be perfect on the first pass.\n          it has to be REPAIRABLE.","originalText":"WRITE     an agent pushes one snapshot  ->  the gateway checks who it is and\n          whether it is allowed to be this loud  ->  Ingest stamps it with OUR\n          clock and hands it to Kafka  ->  202. the request is over in ~30 ms\n          and nothing downstream of Kafka was ever in its way.\n\nFAN OUT   the same event is then read by several consumers, each with its own\n          promise and its own right to fall behind:\n             ARCHIVER      writes it into the lake, where it cannot be edited\n             MATERIALIZER  keeps the newest reading, shouts ONLY on a bucket change\n             ROLLUP        counts the fleet every 5 s, into counters and a cube\n             ALERT ENGINE  holds a rule 3 windows, then pages once, with a count\n             SWEEPER       watches for silence, because silence is an event too\n\nREAD      the admin opens the screen  ->  Query Service reads a handful of\n          numbers  ->  about 115 ms. any slice they ask for next comes from the\n          cube, never from raw events. the tiles then keep moving because the\n          socket sends deltas, each carrying a sequence number.\n\nREPAIR    every 5 minutes the exact count from the cube overwrites the fast\n          counters, so drift is never older than 5 minutes. anything a consumer\n          choked on waits in the dead letter store until a human replays it.\n          anything a consumer missed is still in Kafka for 7 days.\n\n          nothing here has to be perfect on the first pass.\n          it has to be REPAIRABLE.","fontSize":12,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25,"roundness":null,"index":"bta"}],"files":{}}